	"identifying_description": "Leaf of paper with watercolor, opaque watercolor, and brass powder showing lovers under a bright blue sky at center. Tan background is organized by paired bamboo-like verticals and wavy lines with pink and blue roses filling the intervening spaces. Sticks and guards of carved and painted bone with brass paint. Mother-of-pearl pivot and metal rivet.",
	"web_text": "For more than a century, inexpensive ornamental paper, called domino, was used by peasants to decorate their homes. Printed by woodblock and stenciled with color, the papers first served to decorate fireplace mantels, and later, to line drawers and trunks, to cover the end pages of books, to cover walls, and to adorn fan leaves. Many fan leaves produced by other methods imitated the domino style, as seen in this example.",
	"didactic_label": "For more than a century, inexpensive ornamental paper, called domino, was used by peasants to decorate their homes. Printed by woodblock and stenciled with color, the papers first served to decorate fireplace mantels, and later, to line drawers and trunks, to cover the end pages of books, to cover walls, and to adorn fan leaves. Many fan leaves produced by other methods imitated the domino style, as seen in this example.",
	"exhibition_history_text": "Fans of the Eighteenth Century, Textiles Education Gallery, de Young Museum, 3/31/18 - 11/2/18\nFans in Fashion, Legion of Honor, 5/23/81 - 8/30/81",
